# L §8 Refusals outrank holes (tally 2.1.7 R2, issue #141; G7, GAPS l.113): pull[k1] and pull[k2] fall on one day
# with no ties by, and each reads the balance the other changes, so their order changes a result: RUN-TIE (L
# §5.14.2), which refuses each tied instance, so flows of each is refused as bal(konto) is. 2.1.6's refcore refused
# only the balance and printed 700.00 PLN and 0.00 PLN, a silent key order.
=== m.tally
model g7
  tally 2
  on 2026-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source card "invented"

unit PLN: currency minor 0.01

index ks = [k1, k2]

input dday: date

input fee: money PLN

place konto: accounts PLN lives 2026-01-01..

place world: outside lives 2026-01-01..

move pull[k in ks]: transfers
  on dday
  reads konto
  leg cash from konto to world amount bal(konto) - fee

ask b: bal(konto, 2026-03-01)
ask f1: flows(pull[k1], 2026-01-01 .. 2026-12-31)
ask f2: flows(pull[k2], 2026-01-01 .. 2026-12-31)

version v
  cite card
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  dday = 2026-02-01 "c"
  fee = 100.00 PLN "c"
  opening[konto] = 800.00 PLN at 2026-01-01 start "c"
=== expect
b: refused: RUN-TIE pull has a tie at pull[k1], pull[k2] that changes the result, and no ties by
...
f1: refused: RUN-TIE pull has a tie at pull[k1], pull[k2] that changes the result, and no ties by
...
f2: refused: RUN-TIE pull has a tie at pull[k1], pull[k2] that changes the result, and no ties by
...
